From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Playport <dev@playport.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:33:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WOW64: address a 32-bit guest's memory through its window
 inline

A 32-bit guest on the iOS host lives in a 4 GiB window at host base B
(guest g is host B + g). The sub-floor translation of fex-port 0060
added the window offset after a range test at the four AutoTSO choke
points only; every other access (push, pop, call and return, string
ops, atomics, x87, vector element and pair forms) used the guest
address and faulted into Wine's Mach handler, which emulated it: 18.8
million faults in one Portal 2 run, 98% of them the stack ops.

With a window base set (BeginFunction, for a 32-bit block whose window
is the whole 4 GiB), the dispatcher emits:

- loads and stores as [B, wEA, uxtw]: LoadMem/StoreMem take the base
  as a constant register and the guest address as a UXTW register
  offset; LoadMemTSO/StoreMemTSO accept that form too, and the backend
  forms the address in a temporary for the acquire/release encodings,
  which have no register offset;
- push and pop as such a store or load plus an explicit ESP update
  (the Push/Pop ops write back the host pointer they address);
- atomics, CAS pairs, pair, vector element, broadcast, non-temporal,
  masked, x87 80-bit and state-save forms through B + zext32(EA);
- REP STOS with the base in the segment prefix, REP MOVS with the
  base added and taken off the returned pointers, so ESI and EDI stay
  guest addresses;
- full SMC validation reading the code through the window.

The x87 pass forms the split 80-bit store's address in 64 bits: in
32-bit mode it truncated B + EA + 8 to 32 bits.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-04-portal2-inline-translation.md
Offered-upstream: no; the window exists only on the iOS port
